    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The domain of a function refers to the set of all input values that produce real output values. In this case, the input value is specified by the variable $x$.\n\nWe are asked to find the values of $x$ for which $u(x)$ is defined. This means that for any particular value of $x$, we need to ensure that $u(x)$ is a real number.\n\nThe function $u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt x}$ contains a radical function, $\\sqrt x$, which has its domain restricted to non-negative real numbers, or $x \\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, $x$ must be a non-negative number for $u(x)$ to be a real number.\n\nHence, the domain of $u(x)$ is the set of all non-negative real numbers.\n\n$$\\left[0, \\infty\\right)$$",
